# Panchayat Bill
Tamil title: ஊராட்சி மன்றச் சட்ட முன்வடிவு

Rather than try to argue in the manner the minister has adopted, we in the opposition wish, when examining the bill introduced by the ruling party, to look beyond how it will operate in practice and ask what the people expect from you and what you are giving them.

The rural people have been conducting a great agitation, demanding that the deficiencies under which they have lived until now be corrected and that they be given more powers and the facilities needed to administer those powers. That is why many members who spoke in this House said that under British rule all these panchayats had been suppressed in the villages and kept in a condition devoid of national spirit. Some of our friends on this side even asserted a claim that these local councils had existed in ancient Tamil times. The principal reason for raising all this is that a great awakening has now arisen in the countryside. The village people, guided by the sound belief that they must govern themselves, have developed a deep grievance that when they are ruled by someone else, their joys and sorrows receive no attention.

Therefore, whether these rulers call it *Grama Rajya*, or *Rama Rajya*, or, as another Honourable Member said yesterday, declare that they have joined together *Sama Rajya* and *Kama Rajya*, what must be examined is what the people expect through such a polity and what you are giving them through this bill.

**M. R. Kandasami Mudaliar:** On a point of order, sir. Yesterday I said that *Grama Rajya* and *Rama Rajya* had been joined together.

**Mr. Speaker:** There is no difference, is there? The two are one and the same.

**C. N. Annadurai:** I thank our Honourable Member for his correction. I feared that he had gone over to *Kama Rajya*. [Laughter.]

Similarly, the Finance Minister said that there was no need to debate matters in such detail and voiced the fear that speaking in this manner would paralyse the administration. Nor was that all. He said that if attention were diverted elsewhere in this way, the law would be deprived of the proper nourishment it ought to receive. To relieve him of such fears, I say on behalf of my side that we welcome the good work the Government has done through this bill. Our pointing out its faults does not mean that we have forgotten the merits contained in it. Even if the ruling party has not forgotten the bill's defects, but keeps speaking of its merits this way and that, our raising its defects likewise does not mean that we have forgotten its merits.

As I said earlier, when we examine what the village people expect, we find that they seek to govern themselves. They ask for this, and expect an administrative structure to be created for it. They expect all administrative affairs in the villages to be entrusted to them. The local councils, the presidents elected for them, and all executive responsibility must be placed in their own hands. They must be given every right and facility they need so they may conduct their own affairs. It is in the hope that the present Government will make this possible that they seek panchayats.

There is no conference they have failed to convene for this purpose, no resolution they have failed to frame, and no day on which they have failed to seek an interview. Nor have we failed to make promises to them. We say that we have done everything necessary to establish village self-government and have showered them with all the needs, rights, and facilities they require. If, after saying this, we stand before the villagers and, as one Honourable Congress Member requested, go outside this House and praise this law as a measure enacted by the Government, either I or the Leader of the Opposition, V. K. Ramasami Mudaliar, will have to face the people.

When we tell them, "We have granted the rights of village self-government that you have demanded all these years," they may ask, "Instead of allowing us to elect the president of the local council, why have you arranged for the president to be elected from among the members and by those members?" If we ask, "Do you not want the president to be elected by the members?" and they reply, "No," we must then ask why.

They may answer, "Was it not because of this that six people from this village were carried off to Palani? Eight people were taken to Kanchipuram, and six were abducted to Puducherry. After the election, only four of them returned. Did you make this arrangement so that people could be carried away like this?"

What answer are we to give when they ask us this? What explanation shall we offer them? Will our explanation remove their fear and satisfy them? That is what I ask.

---NOTES---
The source plays on `கிராம ராஜ்யம்` (village rule), `ராம ராஜ்யம்` (Rama's rule), `சம ராஜ்யம்` (egalitarian rule), and `காம ராஜ்யம்` (the realm of desire). The indirect-election examples name Palani, Kanchipuram, and Puducherry and retain the printed figures exactly.
